       aamm.  Hence the description of ansi includes aamm.  Numeric capabilities
       are followed by the character “#” and then a positive value.  Thus
       ccoollss, which indicates the number of columns the terminal has, gives the
       value “80” for ansi.  Values for numeric capabilities may be specified
       in decimal, octal, or hexadecimal, using the C programming language
       conventions (e.g., 255, 0377 and 0xff or 0xFF).

       Finally, string valued capabilities, such as eell (clear to end of line
       sequence) are given by the two-character code, an “=”, and then a
       string ending at the next following “,”.

       A number of escape sequences are provided in the string valued capabil‐
       ities for easy encoding of characters there:

       •   Both \\EE and \\ee map to an ESCAPE character,

       •   ^^_xx maps to a control-_x for any appropriate _x, and

       •   the sequences

             \\nn, \\ll, \\rr, \\tt, \\bb, \\ff, and \\ss

           produce

             _n_e_w_l_i_n_e, _l_i_n_e_-_f_e_e_d, _r_e_t_u_r_n, _t_a_b, _b_a_c_k_s_p_a_c_e, _f_o_r_m_-_f_e_e_d, and _s_p_a_c_e,

